%%%
State-space AR-1 trajectory — `markovchain`.

An AR-1 random walk x_t = x_{t-1} + N(0, sigma_step) from a noisy
initial condition x_0 ~ Normal(x0_mean, sigma_init).

Spelled with `markovchain(kernel, init, n)` because the step kernel
means the PREVIOUS state alone. Spec §06 gives `jointchain(M, K1, K2)`
the lowering `a ~ M; b ~ K1(a); c ~ K2([a, b])` — step i binds the
`cat` of every variate to its left — so a chain of three or more
positional components cannot express a prev-only step. `markovchain`
is §06's construct for that feed: "Step i is traj_i ~ kappa(traj_{i-1})".

§06 makes `init` a VALUE, so it carries no density of its own and is
not part of the trajectory; the variate is the array
[traj[1], ..., traj[n]]. Feeding a `draw` as that value puts the noisy
initial condition's variance into every state.

Closed-form calibration (checked in hierarchical-models.test.ts):
  Var(traj[k])          = sigma_init^2 + k * sigma_step^2
  Cov(traj[j], traj[k]) = sigma_init^2 + min(j,k) * sigma_step^2
With sigma_init = 0.1 and sigma_step = 0.5 the variances are
{0.26, 0.51, 0.76, 1.01} and the increments traj[k] - traj[k-1] are
Normal(0, sigma_step), so their sample variance approaches 0.25.
%%%
flatppl_compat = "0.1"

x0_mean = 0.0
sigma_init = 0.1
sigma_step = 0.5

% AR-1 step kernel: x_t | x_{t-1} ~ Normal(x_{t-1}, sigma_step).
step_kernel = kernelof(
  Normal(mu = _prev_, sigma = sigma_step),
  prev = _prev_)

% Noisy initial condition, fed to the chain as §06's `init` value.
x0 = draw(Normal(mu = x0_mean, sigma = sigma_init))

y = markovchain(step_kernel, x0, 4)
